WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Spending time on setting up the environment and installing tools before I can even start testing

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
is the part that I truly dislike.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Luckily, we have things like Kali Linux that comes with a bunch of tools pre-installed.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.000
For web3, we have Ziion and today I'm joined by André from Halborn

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.000
who will show us how we can spend minimum time on setting up the environment for testing web3.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Enjoy!

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Hello André, thank you so much for joining me today.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Today we'll talk about a set of tools that you've built for testing blockchain.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
I struggled a lot when I was reproducing blockchain vulnerabilities

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
with all the setup steps, different blockchains and everything.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
And from what I've heard, you created something that is supposed to solve all these problems.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Can you talk me some about it?

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Yes, of course, Greg. Thank you for having me.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.000
I'm the product owner of Ziion, which is a virtual machine

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
that includes all the tools necessary to do smart contract auditing.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:19.000
You can have a chain in Solidity, EVM, Rust, Solana, Polkadot, Substrate.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:26.000
We have all those protocols already integrated and all the tools to audit them.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Awesome. Sounds awesome.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
So without further ado, can you show me how to find bugs with Zion?

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Of course.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Okay, so we have the N1CTF 2022, which has already been solved and published.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
So we are not going to spoil anything.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:56.000
If you look, you have two folders. One is the server and the other one is the solve.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
And then you have a script that simply compiles the solve and just runs it.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:09.000
So if we look into the framework,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:18.000
we can run low-hanging fruit tools, for example, to find vulnerabilities in the crate,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.000
in the dependencies, or things like that.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.000
We have Cargo audit, we have Cargo geiger for anything unsafe.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
We have Siderophile for potential fuzzing targets,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
even though a manual review is always better for this.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.000
We have many other tools that are available, like Soteria, you have other things like that.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
So let's do, for example, Cargo Audit.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:55.000
And we'll see that it will find some vulnerabilities in the crates that this code uses,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:02.000
which is pretty common, simply because you have dependencies that have some vulnerabilities

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.000
doesn't mean necessarily that your contract is insecure.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
For example, you have this one, the time, which is a potential segfault,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:19.000
which is quite high vulnerability, yet doesn't mean that you are vulnerable to it.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.000
You have other ones, race conditions, etc.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.000
But for each of them, you need to ensure that by checking the advisory,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.000
that your contract is not vulnerable through the dependency.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Yeah, that's great. Tools are always good starting points.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.000
But I imagine if it's not the most basic CTF task or in the work itself,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:50.000
we do need to do manual analysis. Do we have some tools in ZIION to also do this?

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Of course. So, yeah, in most contract audits,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.000
this is just the first few tools that you run to get quick, low-hanging fruits

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:06.000
that are pretty standard into a report. But we have VS Code, actually VS Codium.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:16.000
So that way it is open source and with a bit less tracking than the Microsoft version of VS Code.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:22.000
You can see here by opening the CTF that you have a library.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.000
You also have a main contract there.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:34.000
And basically, most of the time of the smart contract auditor will be spent in Codium

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.000
doing manual code review because even though AI tools are big now, etc.,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:51.000
nothing beats the human yet. So here you can see that you have a main program that is a server.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:57.000
It is listening on the port 8080, creates a connection.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:08.000
You can see that it initialized environment, mint some token and register an employee A,

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.000
for example, from the organization product.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:23.000
It then deposits a thousand token, mints a thousand token and deposits 500 token into that account

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:29.000
for the employee A.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:32.000
This is the account we don't have control over. Is it correct?

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:38.000
Exactly. This is the account that is created automatically by the program right now.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.000
We have no access to it. We must create a new account.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:54.000
Once the setup is done for that account, we have now the player account that has 100 tokens.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.000
100 tokens is available to the player and the goal is to get more tokens, obviously.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.000
How do we do it?

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.000
That's a great question. How do you find vulnerabilities in contracts in general?

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.000
First, you have to check the different functions of the contracts.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:19.000
You can see that there are three main functions.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:24.000
One is register function after the initialization, of course.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.000
You can see that it requires the employee ID to be a certain length,

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.000
the organization to be a certain length too.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Then it ensures that this already exists. Otherwise, it is duplicated.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.000
Otherwise, it says that it's a duplicate employee.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:56.000
Then we have the deposit function.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Then we can deposit tokens to that employee.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:06.000
You can see that it requires that the employee is already in the system

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:13.000
and it checks that the employee record, the organization and the ID is already there.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Then it does the transfer and it ensures that the vault amount is superior

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:25.000
to the amount that we are actually depositing.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.000
Then you have the withdraw function.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.000
Same thing, it does the checks to be sure that this employee exists.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:41.000
It ensures that we can't withdraw more than what the vault has in the correct order.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:48.000
It checks for the different seeds and then it withdraws.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:56.000
If we go check the struct of those functions, you can see the register.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.000
It registers into the catalog the seeds of the user.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.000
It checks that that's the employee record.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.000
Now we're in the struct for the deposit.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.000
We see that they have initialization if needed.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.000
It creates the account if it's not already created.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:21.000
It seeds the account as organization name as bytes and employee ID as bytes.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.000
What's a seed? What is it used for?

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:33.000
The seed is used for the creation of the PDA, which is a program-derived address.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:39.000
Basically, in Solana, everything is an account.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:47.000
The program has a PDA to do its bidding in terms of transfers.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.000
You can see you have different constraints here.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:56.000
The employee record must be the organization name and it must be also the ID.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:03.000
We can't really fake the PDA.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.000
We can't really fake the token itself.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:18.000
There are some restrictions which prevent it to simply allow us to hack this.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:32.000
If we run, for example, the script which simply runs the solve,

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:37.000
we can see that the player has 100 tokens.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.000
That's our basic amount.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:46.000
Right now, we don't have the flag or anything simply because we don't have more than 100 tokens.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:58.000
In order to do that, we have to check how can we withdraw more without encountering those constraints.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:04.000
The key here is in the way that the seed is generated.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:12.000
The seed is generated simply with our name as bytes and employee ID as bytes concatenated together.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:20.000
If we predict the seed, does it automatically mean we can withdraw money from a different account?

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.000
Withdraw tokens, sorry.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:28.000
Exactly. We basically do some kind of seed collision by doing that.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:43.000
You can see that the original seed when you look here is based on product and employee in the program by default.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:50.000
To solve that, we need to reproduce this string, product and employee together.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:56.000
Well, that's actually quite simple and at the same time a bit sneaky.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:02.000
And also something that the automatic tools wouldn't find.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:12.000
You simply need to first find the program address which is with that seed product employee.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:22.000
And then into the solving program, which is here, you can see that that's where we are.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:27.000
We have employee B and then our instructions go here.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:32.000
Well, we don't want to be employee B. We want the funds from employee A.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:38.000
Yeah, but there was a check for if employee ID is unique, no?

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:47.000
Actually, yes, it is. But the seed itself is what allows the transfer.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:54.000
So here, if you change the strings to do this, it will work.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:03.000
Or you can also simply do that and it will work because the seed in the end is the same when you add those two strings together.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:12.000
Yeah, because there's a check if the employee ID is unique, but there's no check if these two things concatenated.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.000
Exactly. And that's exactly why it is vulnerable.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:24.000
So if we complete the withdrawal function, we saw how the struct is and what are the parameters.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:34.000
I'm pasting here the different parameters for the account withdrawal, which is pretty standard there.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:38.000
And here we're going to withdraw 500 tokens.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:44.000
We could withdraw less if we want, but we know that this employee A has 500 tokens.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.000
Here, I just save the files and you can see that there's an error here.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:13:01.000
It's simply because we need to clone those variables because in Rust, you have to borrow or clone the different variables.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:16.000
Now that it's saved, this is good. And if we run it, it should normally bypass the security and give us the flag, hopefully.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:27.000
And as you can see, this time we have 600 tokens and the flag is flag{downloadZion_@_ziion.org}.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:33.000
That's awesome. That's great. And speaking of download Ziion, how does this work?

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.000
Is it the Docker image? Is it the virtual machine?

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:42.000
Where do people go to install this and basically start testing web-free vulnerabilities?

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:47.000
Sure. So we can download a VM directly. I will show you.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:53.000
So we go to the website, we click on download, we choose the architecture that we want.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:02.000
For example, Intel AMD, and we want from the VirtualBox one because that's what we have installed.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:07.000
Double click on it, it launches VirtualBox. You can check the different parameters.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:13.000
You can adjust how many CPUs or RAM you want to put to assign to this VM, for example.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:20.000
And once that's fully imported, you can simply just start it.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:24.000
That's simple and easy, just like I like it.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:32.000
Yep. You simply double click on things and somehow it all happens.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:40.000
Awesome. Looks really good. Thank you so much for joining me today and explaining to us how to use this.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:48.000
I will definitely use it the next time I'm explaining or looking for web3 vulnerabilities.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:55.000
For people, they go to ziion.org to install this. Is this correct?

00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:01.000
Yes, it's correct. You go to ziion.org and you can click on the download button just as we showed earlier.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:08.000
You can also register on a newsletter by going on to halborn.com and subscribing there.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:14.000
We also have a blog on the ziion.org website, so you can check your content from time to time there.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:18.000
Awesome. Thank you again for joining me today.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.000
My pleasure. Thank you for having me, Greg.

